-- title:
--   A Hecke generator up to bounded index for inertia displacements
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and let $B$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ lying over $2$, in the sense that $2$ belongs to the non-units of $B$; the Hecke algebra $\mathtt{HeckeAlg} = \mathrm{MvPolynomial}\ \mathtt{Nat.Primes}\ \mathbf Z$ acts on $\mathtt{JZero}\ p$, the degree-zero divisor class group of the level-$p$ modular function field over $\overline{\mathbf Q}$, through `heckeModuleBar`. Write $D$ for the subgroup of $\mathtt{JZero}\ p$ generated by the displacements $\sigma\cdot x - x$ with $\sigma$ in the image of the inertia subgroup of $B$ over $\mathbf Q$ and $x$ annihilated by some power $\mathfrak m^M$ of the Eisenstein ideal $\mathfrak m =$ `eisensteinMaximalIdeal p 2` (the contraction along `eisensteinEval` of the ideal $(2)$ of $\mathbf Z$), and write $L$ for the intersection of $\mathtt{TateModule}\ 2\ (\mathtt{JZero}\ p)$ — the sequences $x : \mathbf N \to \mathtt{JZero}\ p$ with $2^n\cdot x_n = 0$ and $2\cdot x_{n+1} = x_n$ for all $n$ — with the sequences all of whose components lie in $D$. The assertion is that there are natural numbers $a, b$ and an element $s_0 \in L$ such that: (i) for every $m$ and every $t \in \mathtt{HeckeAlg}$, if every $s \in L$ satisfies $t\cdot s = 2^{m+b}\cdot l$ for some $l \in L$, then $t$ annihilates every $\mathfrak m^M$-torsion element of $\mathtt{JZero}\ p$ (any $M$) that is killed by $2^m$; and (ii) for every $m$ and every $s \in L$ there is $t \in \mathtt{HeckeAlg}$ with $t\cdot s_0 \in L$ and $2^a\cdot s - t\cdot s_0 = 2^m\cdot l$ for some $l \in L$.
--
--   This is the rank-one statement, with bounded defects $a$ and $b$, for the inertia-displacement part of the $2$-adic Tate module of the Jacobian of the level-$p$ modular curve at a place above $2$, in the style of Mazur's analysis of the Eisenstein ideal: clause (ii) says that $s_0$ generates this part up to a fixed power of $2$ and $2$-adic approximation, while clause (i) converts near-divisibility of a Hecke operator on it into annihilation of Eisenstein torsion. It is used to produce an idempotent-tower generator in [`ModularCurve.exists_nsmul_generator_idempotent_tower_heckeAlg_tateModule_inf_pi_closure_inertia_smul_sub`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_nsmul_generator_idempotent_tower_heckeAlg_tateModule_inf_pi_closure_inertia_smul_sub).
